From 6ee8d494bc89b3586b1bdf5e75a540721036d549 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: fmillion <47171880+fmillion@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:17:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 37/42] ACPI: PM: s2idle: honour _S0W for wake-IRQ devices
 without that D-state

acpi_dev_pm_get_state() falls back to D3cold when _SxW returns a D-state the
device does not implement. For a device that may wake the system from s2idle
through a wake IRQ (GpioInt/Interrupt ExclusiveAndWake), D3cold is exactly the
state it cannot wake from - and on the ThinkPad X1 Fold Gen 1 the Bluetooth
controller's _PS3 asserts its kill line, so it is powered off and comes back
in its bootloader (_S0W = 2, no _PS2).

In that case use the deepest implemented state not deeper than _S0W (here D0).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/acpi/device_pm.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/acpi/device_pm.c b/drivers/acpi/device_pm.c
index 4e0583274..34c20012c 100644
--- a/drivers/acpi/device_pm.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/device_pm.c
@@ -756,6 +756,19 @@ static int acpi_dev_pm_get_state(struct device *dev, struct acpi_device *adev,
 			if (has_sxd && target_state > ACPI_STATE_S0)
 				d_max = d_min;
 		} else if (ACPI_SUCCESS(status) && ret <= ACPI_STATE_D3_COLD) {
+			if (!adev->power.states[ret].flags.valid &&
+			    target_state == ACPI_STATE_S0 && dev->power.wakeirq) {
+				/*
+				 * _S0W names the deepest state from which the device
+				 * can still signal wakeup through its wake IRQ. If that
+				 * state is not implemented (e.g. _S0W = 2 without
+				 * _PS2), use the deepest implemented state above it
+				 * instead of D3cold, from which it could not wake (and
+				 * which may cut its power through _PS3).
+				 */
+				while (ret > d_min && !adev->power.states[ret].flags.valid)
+					ret--;
+			}
 			/* Fall back to D3cold if ret is not a valid state. */
 			if (!adev->power.states[ret].flags.valid)
 				ret = ACPI_STATE_D3_COLD;
